## Scientific invariants

- Before interpreting a result, name the mathematical or physical object, conventions, independent variable, boundary or initial data, approximation domain, normalization, and observable.
- A local relation, matching condition, fit, or implementation smoke test does not by itself determine a global or observable scientific signal.
- Separate numerical error from physical, model, data, and statistical uncertainty.
- Average amplitudes, states, samples, or observables only according to an explicit physical or statistical model; numerical quadrature and truncation are not decoherence or uncertainty.
- A pole, optimum, or small residual does not establish a large observable effect. Report the residue, sensitivity, degeneracy, or decision-relevant response as appropriate.
- Generated plots, arrays, PDFs, caches, and checkpoints are never scientific evidence inputs by default.
- Do not add an observational, population, sensitivity, or constraint statement until the applicable source, environment, response, averaging, covariance, selection, and nuisance semantics are explicit.

## Research workflow

Use `$orient-scientific-project`, `$explore-scientific-ideas`, `$research-scientific-literature`, `$derive-scientific-results`, `$implement-scientific-computations`, `$manage-manuscript-citations`, `$verify-manuscript-results`, and `$edit-scientific-manuscripts` according to the scientific question. These skills are available routes, not a required sequence. Work may remain explanatory, exploratory, analytic, literature-based, computational, or manuscript-focused when that directly answers the question.

Use `$calibrate-scientific-evidence` when physical and mathematical routes diverge, when direct calculation, analytic estimation, formal proof, or infrastructure work must be reranked, or before a third consecutive same-branch milestone that has not changed an observable, tested a decisive assumption, or discriminated a regime; keep mathematical and physical routes independent unless an explicit observable bridge makes one a prerequisite for the other.

Prefer answering the scientific question directly over creating repository artifacts. Create or update durable artifacts only when they preserve evidence, support reuse, or record a decision that will matter later. Update `RESEARCH_PLAN.md` only when the central question, candidate ordering, hypothesis, or durable decision changes; update topic packets and `SCIENTIFIC_PROGRESS.md` only when their scientific status or routing actually changes.

## Computation, configuration, and verification

Follow project-declared resource guards for long or high-memory work. During adoption, preserve established guard commands and thresholds; when none are declared, estimate resources before escalating and do not invent project-specific limits.

The `calculations/{core,models,workflows,cli}` tree is a promotion path, not a requirement to fill layers. Keep cheap exploratory scripts under ignored `scratch/` while equations or observables are unsettled. Promote stable work using dependency direction `cli -> workflows -> models/core`; keep `core` independent of project models and interfaces.

Match configuration and verification effort to the strength, reuse, and decision relevance of the claim. Exploratory reasoning and scratch calculations may keep inputs and assumptions locally without a full configuration or verification stack. When inputs become reusable or support a promoted claim, create an active configuration that defines their scientific object, units, schema, boundaries, controls, provenance, and observable tolerances. Changed scientific inputs must invalidate incompatible checkpoints.

Unit tests may check implementation behavior. A promoted derived, numerical, or otherwise executable project-level scientific claim needs a focused `verification/verify_*.py` script that recomputes the named identity or benchmark from active inputs and fails nonzero with an actionable residual or predicate. Literature and conceptual claims instead retain their appropriate source, argument, assumptions, and validity limits. Keep online source checks separate from default verification.

## Manuscript and bibliography guidance

- Recover include order from `{{TEX_ROOT}}` before editing a section.
- Read only the relevant manuscript context and topic packet, then inspect the source or verifier.
- Preserve equations, labels, citations, notation, numerical values, caveats, and validity domains unless the task explicitly changes them.
- Manuscript drafting may precede verification when provisional statements are labeled clearly. Do not present a derived or numerical statement as established until its evidence and validity limits are adequate.
- Treat section files as integration targets; their existence is not claim evidence.
- Verify DOI/arXiv identity and source-to-claim rationale in `CITATION_PLAN.md` before adding an entry to `{{BIB_FILE}}`.

